# Badge

A small inline label marking the state or category of the thing it
sits next to.

## When to use

- Marking a record's state in a list or header: "Active", "Overdue",
  "Stale".
- Categorising: team names, episode types, counts.

## When not to use

- As a button — badges are not interactive. If it needs a click, it
  is a `button` (size `sm`) or a link.
- For long text — a badge holds a word or two.
- As an alert — page-level states use `alert`; a badge marks one
  record, not the page.

## Anatomy and options

A badge is one element — write it inline; there is no partial.

```erb
<span class="badge" data-variant="secondary">Active</span>
```

The markup is the whole API: the `badge` class plus a `data-variant`.
Omit `data-variant` for the default (primary) badge. Add any extra
utility classes or HTML attributes directly on the `<span>`.

<%= embed :badge, :variants %>

`secondary` and `outline` are the neutral workhorses (the most-used
in the production apps) — category labels, counts. The clinical
variants (`success` `warning` `info` `critical` `stale`) carry state
meaning from the colour tokens.

## Do and don't

- Do pick state variants by meaning, not appearance — `warning`
  because the data needs attention (see the *Colour* foundations
  page).
- Do use `stale` for data past its freshness window — that token
  exists precisely for this.
- Don't put state colour on every badge; neutral badges keep state
  badges meaningful.
- Don't encode state *only* in colour — the badge text must carry
  the meaning on its own ("Overdue", not a bare red dot).

## Accessibility and content

- Renders a `<span>` — no role needed; the text is read inline with
  the surrounding content.
- One or two words, sentence case, no punctuation.
- Because the text carries the meaning, colour-blind users lose
  nothing.
